#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Sets up Unio's answering service (runner/unio-inbox) on this computer, one
service per workspace, and manages it afterwards. People run it as
`unio runner`, through bin/unio:

    unio runner setup [--workspace org_<id>] [--api URL] [--token-stdin]
                      [--repos-json FILE] [--no-service] [--yes]
    unio runner status [--workspace org_<id>] [--json]
    unio runner start|stop|restart [--workspace org_<id>]
    unio runner logs [--workspace org_<id>] [-n N] [-f]
    unio runner uninstall [--workspace org_<id>] [--yes]
    unio runner update

--workspace may be left out when this computer has one workspace's token.
It exits 0 when done, 1 on a problem and 2 on a usage error, and without a
terminal it asks nothing: what a question would settle must come as an option.

unio-inbox owns where a workspace's profile lives (--print-paths) and whether
it works (--check): this file asks it and never re-derives either, so the two
cannot disagree. The service runs `<python> runner/unio-inbox --workspace
<org>` from the clone this file sits in, under systemd --user on Linux, a
LaunchAgent on macOS and a Task Scheduler task on Windows; the definitions
come from runner/services/.

Python 3.9 or later, standard library only. Everything it prints is Brazilian
Portuguese, in install.sh's voice. A token never reaches argv or a log.
"""
